  "body": "### Summary\n\nAs a reader, I want a message made of many crossed tags (`[u][b]x[/u][/b]` on every line, the shape HFR's own toolbar produces when a member underlines then bolds) to render in time proportional to its length, so that a long post or its preview does not freeze the reader.\n\n### Scope\n\nFound by the LOT-097 code review (ADR 0014 §4, PR #444). HFR writes crossed tags crossed (`<span class=\"u\"><strong>x</span></strong>`, `R41a` → `R20`); the reader parses a message twice (`MessageBlockParser.contentHTML`, then `MessageContentNormalizer.normalize(contentHTML:)`), and the second parse reopens the `<strong>` around everything that follows each crossed pair, so the `<strong>` count doubles and the normalizer's time grows superlinearly:\n\n| Size of `[u][b]x[/u][/b]\\r\\n` repeated | normalizer (debug build) | page parse |\n|---|---|---|\n| 32 KB | 1.3 s | 0.4 s |\n| 64 KB | 4.2 s | 0.7 s |\n| 128 KB | 14.2 s | 1.3 s |\n\nThe output is right (it is what the browser shows for the posted message); only the time is at stake. LOT-097's renderer reproduces the reader's path on purpose, so the preview pays the same (LOT-152 renders off the main actor and debounced). The normalizer walks an explicit stack (LOT-034), so the cost is in SwiftSoup's tree builder, in the reconstruction of active formatting elements, or in the serialization of the doubled tree.\n\n### Sources\n\n`Packages/HFRKit/Sources/HFRParsing/Topic/MessageContentNormalizer.swift`, `MessageBlockParser.swift`; the shape in `HFRBBCodeWriterTests.linearWork` (last of `shapes(_:)`); `R20` `para2666649` for a real message with 13 crossed pairs.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Measure where the time goes (tree builder, adoption agency, serialization) on the shape above at 32, 64 and 128 KB.\n- [ ] Either bound it (a repair pass before the second parse, or a cap on the reopening) or record the limit in LOT-034's spec and HFR-PROTOCOL with the measurement, and say what the reader does with a message that exceeds it.\n- [ ] A test on the shape with a bound that holds on CI.",
